Starting with the Top Customers query, this is definitely one of the stronger ones. 
The calculation of lifetime value using quantity, unit price, and discount shows a good understanding of how revenue should actually be calculated.
It’s not just a basic sum, but something that reflects real business logic. 
The joins are straightforward and correct, and the use of TOP 5 combined with ordering makes the result immediately useful.
Overall, this query is clear and does exactly what it’s supposed to do without unnecessary complexity.

The Inactive Customers query is also well handled. 
Using MAX(order_date) to determine the last activity is the right approach, and including the condition for NULL values shows attention to edge cases. 
This ensures that customers who have never placed an order are not missed, which is important in this type of analysis. 
The logic is clean, and the use of LEFT JOIN is appropriate here. 
This query reflects a good understanding of how to work with missing data and time-based conditions.

For the Products with No Sales query, the approach is simple but effective. 
Using a LEFT JOIN and checking for NULL is the correct and standard way to solve this type of problem. 
There’s no overcomplication, which is actually a positive here. The query is easy to read and does exactly what is expected, making it reliable and easy to maintain.

The Top 5 Sales Reps query follows a similar pattern to the Top Customers query, which is a good thing.
Reusing the same revenue calculation logic keeps everything consistent across the analysis. 
The grouping and ordering are correct, and the result is clear and meaningful.
This kind of consistency across queries shows a good level of organization and understanding.
